#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287422 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8c990d69-54da-5541-948f-0d5eff96feb5" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the X-Intercepts of a Parabola Given Its Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1b34bf13-64ca-557f-a0a2-5636a64c6009" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$8$ and $-1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287424} {:answer/id #uuid "b917d03e-e0f2-5f50-b6b3-8fcee0175693" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$7$ and $-1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287425} {:answer/id #uuid "473f69b9-9812-5ee9-a288-68c3ed1d0bbd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$9$ and $-2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287426} {:answer/id #uuid "33b53780-aa11-56b8-9508-05c03d806b58" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$7$ and $-2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287427} {:answer/id #uuid "684e3212-593c-5c35-887a-80e212357ba4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$8$ and $-3$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287428}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b917d03e-e0f2-5f50-b6b3-8fcee0175693" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$7$ and $-1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287425} :answer-field/id #uuid "e62ee236-d0f5-56d1-ab73-e2ba41ae5b80" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287423}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1270303e-0109-58be-ac65-6aecbfb8540f" :question/math-academy-id "q-14050" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14005477/assets/0470661491c0d9d44ecbbc3c87af70d4ff334f1ffd2ca56b68bd4c126f3d88de.png)\n\nWhat are the $x$-intercepts of the parabola shown above?" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find the equation of the parabola. From the diagram, we see that the parabola has a vertex at $(3,-4)$ and passes through the point $(0,-\\frac{7}{4}).$\n\nThe general standard equation of an up- or down-opening parabola with vertex $(h,k)$ is given by\n\n$$\n(x-h)^{2}=4p(y-k).\n$$\n\nSubstituting $(h,k)=(3,-4)$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-3)^{2} & =4p(y+4).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find $p.$ Since the parabola passes through the point $(0,-\\frac{7}{4}),$ we substitute $x=0$ and $y=-\\frac{7}{4}$ into the equation and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(0-3)^{2} & =4p(-\\frac{7}{4}+4) \\\\ 9 & =4p(\\frac{9}{4}) \\\\ p & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the parabola is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-3)^{2} & =4(y+4).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhen the parabola intersects the $x$-axis, we must have $y=0.$ So, we substitute $y=0$ into the equation of the parabola and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-3)^{2} & =4(0+4) \\\\ (x-3)^{2} & =16 \\\\ x-3 & =\\pm 4 \\\\ x & =\\pm 4+3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $x$-intercepts are $7$ and $-1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097028 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8c990d69-54da-5541-948f-0d5eff96feb5" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the X-Intercepts of a Parabola Given Its Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0196dd01-6e9a-51b5-b540-feb8cdec58fd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "± 3\\sqrt{3} - 5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186094575} {:answer/id #uuid "1eae5baf-0ef7-5ad8-bd9e-53f057830198" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "± 2\\sqrt{3} - 5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186094576} {:answer/id #uuid "2fcec640-b57c-5181-93b6-ddf79f54d2d2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "± \\sqrt{3} - 5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186094577} {:answer/id #uuid "3bf91607-f67c-520c-9e3c-c7df50c1d77b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "± 2\\sqrt{3} - 6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186094578} {:answer/id #uuid "6c967b6d-586b-599a-94b6-49a0dd82631a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "± 2\\sqrt{3} + 3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186094579}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1eae5baf-0ef7-5ad8-bd9e-53f057830198" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "± 2\\sqrt{3} - 5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186094576} :answer-field/id #uuid "843cc523-38da-5727-a3aa-f35516809c00" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186094574}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9747782d-b4d5-539b-ba0f-f2d6a1a22fcb" :question/math-academy-id "q-14052" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14005477/assets/f2de46decf74a41e6375325e6eeb015113311b00300eb639d0f1f85a9f779337.png)\n\nWhat are the $x$-intercepts of the parabola shown above?" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find the equation of the parabola. From the diagram, we see that the parabola has a vertex at $(-5,-6)$ and passes through the point $(-1,2).$\n\nThe general standard equation of an up- or down-opening parabola with vertex $(h,k)$ is given by\n\n$$\n(x-h)^{2}=4p(y-k).\n$$\n\nSubstituting $(h,k)=(-5,-6)$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+5)^{2} & =4p(y+6).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find $p.$ Since the parabola passes through the point $(-1,2),$ we substitute $x=-1$ and $y=2$ into the equation and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-1+5)^{2} & =4p(2+6) \\\\ 16 & =4p(8) \\\\ p & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the parabola is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+5)^{2} & =2(y+6).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhen the parabola intersects the $x$-axis, we must have $y=0.$ So, we substitute $y=0$ into the equation of the parabola and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+5)^{2} & =2(0+6) \\\\ (x+5)^{2} & =12 \\\\ x+5 & =\\pm \\sqrt{12} \\\\ x & =\\pm 2\\sqrt{3}-5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $x$-intercepts are $\\pm 2\\sqrt{3}-5.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287429 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5e182f71-db24-5c29-9c8b-2fc4b5ab5dd8" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Y-Intercepts of a Parabola Given Its Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "38592871-f043-5184-b2b4-327ef67c82d6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$2$ and $7$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287431} {:answer/id #uuid "12257405-0e0c-5f7d-83b3-2dbf478154da" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$2$ and $6$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287432} {:answer/id #uuid "6b453dca-9ef9-5131-b9a1-3138abe80db2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$1$ and $6$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287433} {:answer/id #uuid "a400110b-5e31-5036-98bb-f3b44593a7a6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$3$ and $5$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287434} {:answer/id #uuid "440caec2-dec0-51d1-b80b-187b7d5464a3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$1$ and $7$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287435}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "12257405-0e0c-5f7d-83b3-2dbf478154da" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$2$ and $6$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287432} :answer-field/id #uuid "6342a59e-8cce-585f-b1a4-39040a9eef59" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287430}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0ecc876e-1e30-5d04-b02b-e0d45341e6f7" :question/math-academy-id "q-14241" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14005477/assets/9b7acbd4f24b6bbd20dd00fc042405324c069d28db0102c2cd7dd49e6b3bbccd.png)\n\nWhat are the $y$-intercepts of the parabola shown above?" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find the equation of the parabola. From the diagram, we see that the parabola has a vertex at $(2,4)$ and passes through the point $(-6,8).$\n\nThe general standard equation of a left- or right-opening parabola with vertex at $(h,k)$ is given by\n\n$$\n(y-k)^{2}=4p(x-h),\n$$\n\nand substituting $(h,k)=(2,4)$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n(y-4)^{2}=4p(x-2).\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find $p.$ Since the parabola passes through the point $(-6,8),$ we substitute $x=-6$ and $y=8$ into the equation and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(8-4)^{2} & =4p(-6-2) \\\\ 16 & =4p(-8) \\\\ p & =-\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the parabola is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y-4)^{2} & =-2(x-2).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhen the parabola intersects the $y$-axis, we must have $x=0.$ So, we substitute $x=0$ into the equation of the parabola and solve for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y-4)^{2} & =-2(0-2) \\\\ (y-4)^{2} & =4 \\\\ y-4 & =\\pm 2 \\\\ y & =\\pm 2+4 \\\\ y & =2,6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $y$-intercepts are $2$ and  $6.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287436 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8c990d69-54da-5541-948f-0d5eff96feb5" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the X-Intercepts of a Parabola Given Its Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e136ee47-27fc-560d-aee3-61dca23f5f29" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287438}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e136ee47-27fc-560d-aee3-61dca23f5f29" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287438} :answer-field/id #uuid "b0fa099f-8147-557e-813d-150ebc4c0b8e" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287437}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "72b27de8-4c9e-5d25-95fa-3b9ff814dba0" :question/math-academy-id "q-251692" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14005477/assets/8b48ef824df51aa680a727dc46d04e0936f61729475026b90378e153db503c76.png)\n\nThe value of the $x$-intercept of the parabola shown above is{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find the equation of the parabola. From the diagram, we see that the parabola has a vertex at $(-6,2)$ and passes through the point $(-7,4).$\n\nThe general standard equation of a left- or right-opening parabola with vertex $(h,k)$ is given by\n\n$$\n(y-k)^{2}=4p(x-h).\n$$\n\nSubstituting $(h,k)=(-6,2)$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n(y-2)^{2}=4p(x+6).\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find $p.$ Since the parabola passes through the point $(-7,4),$ we substitute $x=-7$ and $y=4$ into the equation and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(4-2)^{2} & =4p(-7+6) \\\\ (2)^{2} & =4p\\cdot (-1) \\\\ 4 & =-4p \\\\ p & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the parabola is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y-2)^{2} & =-4(x+6).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhen the parabola intersects the $x$-axis, we must have $y=0.$ So, we substitute $y=0$ into the equation of the parabola and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(0+2)^{2} & =-4(x+6) \\\\ 4 & =-4(x+6) \\\\ -\\frac{4}{4} & =x+6 \\\\ x & =-7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $x$-intercept is $-7.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287439 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5e182f71-db24-5c29-9c8b-2fc4b5ab5dd8" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Y-Intercepts of a Parabola Given Its Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "879bf4d5-5247-5f7b-8a67-61010f549506" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287441}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "879bf4d5-5247-5f7b-8a67-61010f549506" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287441} :answer-field/id #uuid "17c2a04e-38dc-5402-9867-7517c2eeb3ea" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287440}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e981b6dc-b9b2-5bb8-9cef-bfdc3600a8d8" :question/math-academy-id "q-251800" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14005477/assets/b03beb34efa3fef7aaf18a879eb628748d86dc1b47eb4b45f60c4d02935e372b.png)\n\nThe value of the $y$-intercept of the parabola shown above  is{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find the equation of the parabola. From the diagram, we see that the parabola has a vertex at $(4,-3)$ and passes through the point $(10,6).$\n\nThe general standard equation of an up- or down-opening parabola with vertex at $(h,k)$ is given by\n\n$$\n(x-h)^{2}=4p(y-k),\n$$\n\nand substituting $(h,k)=(4,-3)$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n(x-4)^{2}=4p(y+3).\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find $p.$ Since the parabola passes through the point $(10,6),$ we substitute $x=10$ and $y=6$ into the equation and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(10-4)^{2} & =4p(6+3) \\\\ 36 & =4p(9) \\\\ p & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the parabola is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-4)^{2} & =4(y+3).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhen the parabola intersects the $y$-axis, we must have $x=0.$ So, we substitute $x=0$ into the equation of the parabola and solve for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(0-4)^{2} & =4(y+3) \\\\ 16 & =4(y+3) \\\\ 4 & =y+3 \\\\ y & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $y$-intercept is $1.$"}]}
